on this Memorial Day an effort is  underway to have the nation's highest  military award bestowed on an Air Force  sergeant killed in a controversial  operation in Afghanistan 16 years ago  national security correspondent Jennifer  Griffin has the story from the Pentagon  reminded at a Medal of Honor ceremony  last week the president honored Navy  SEAL Britz lubinski for his actions in  Afghanistan shortly after 9/11 a failed  attempt to set up an observation post  nearly 11,000 feet above sea level over  an area where al-qaeda fighters were  operating Air Force tech sergeant John  Chapman was among the seven US service  members killed that day after a series  of mistakes on what is now known as  Roberts Ridge Chapman was shot returning  to find Navy SEAL Neil Roberts who  slipped and fell out of their helicopter  initially thought to have been fatally  injured Chapman was left by the  retreating SEALs but appeared to fight  on alone  the Air Force which has not received a  Medal of Honor since Vietnam has been  pushing to award Chapman the nation's  highest medal for valor we know now from  those predator feeds that John Chapman  was not dead that he got back up he kept  fighting  he killed additional enemy forces for  years former Air Force secretary Deborah  James pushed for Chapman's Air Force  cross to be upgraded he paved the way  for a safe the safe entry of the  reinforcing forces that were coming in  to follow on to the battle new evidence  the result of improved technology and  analysis of images fed back from a drone  flying above the attack site suggests  that Chapman fought on for more than an  hour after the seals left him the Air  Force does not blame Team Leader Brits  lubinski I think he did his absolute  level best on the worst day of his life  former army Delta Force operator Jim  Reese was in the Operations Center in  Afghanistan watching the video live  being privy to those to those feeds that  I was privy to during the operation that  night  seeing someone shooting when those  Rangers are coming in  yes I believe Chapman was alive the  seals say they did not know Chapman was  alive when they retreated  I do believe chappy was fighting up  there his resiliency you know he  probably passed out initially came back  up what Chapman did was above and beyond  because he was the last one on that  mountain so you know as we say though I  be the lone survivor at the Pentagon  Jennifer Griffin Fox News
 

